{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>These shall stand to bless.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: the blessing was ״ברוך האיש אשר לא יעשה פסל ומסכה״ — 'Blessed is the man who does not make a carved or molten image'; the curse as written. And on the blessing were six sons of the noble women, and on the curse were the sons of the maidservants; and since they were only Dan and Naphtali, Gad and Asher, it was necessary to take two from the sons of Leah — Reuben the firstborn and Zebulun the youngest — so as to complete them to six. And the reason Simeon was counted among those for the blessing is that here eleven curses were written, and He did not wish to curse him and say 'cursed' over against him; and when Moses established a blessing in the parashah of וזאת הברכה for all the tribes, he did not establish one for Simeon, because he was not worthy of blessing on account of the sin of Zimri; and since it was not in his heart to bless him, for he was not worthy of blessing, he did not wish to curse him here and say 'cursed' over against him — for if so, he would have had no remedy. And our Rabbis of blessed memory have already interpreted that even though he did not bless him explicitly, he hinted at it in the blessing of Judah, as it says (Deuteronomy 33:7): ״שמע ה׳ קול יהודה״ — for the inheritance of the sons of Simeon was within the inheritance of the sons of Judah. And the reason Reuben was counted among the curse is not for his condemnation, but it appears to me that it was a complete vindication regarding the matter of Bilhah, and to declare that he did not sin with his father's wife — for behold, he pronounces 'cursed be he who lies with his father's wife'; and from this they said in the Midrash (Genesis 49:4): ״יצועי עלה״ — his father Jacob said to him: 'My son, you have no remedy until there comes that one in whom it is written (Exodus 19:3) ״עלה״'; once Moses came and stationed the priests, the Levites, on Mount Gerizim and on Mount Ebal, and stationed Reuben among the curse, all Israel knew that Reuben was innocent. And so they said, our Rabbis of blessed memory: whoever says Reuben sinned is merely mistaken. And how did they stand, these for the blessing and these for the curse? Thus our Rabbis of blessed memory taught in tractate Sotah: six tribes went up to the top of Mount Gerizim and six tribes went up to the top of Mount Ebal, and the priests and the Levites stood below in the middle — the priests surrounding the Ark and the Levites surrounding the priests, and the Israelites on this side and on that, as it says (Joshua 8:33): ״וכל ישראל וזקניו ושוטרים ושופטיו עומדים מזה ומזה לארון נגד הכהנים הלוים״; they turned their faces toward Mount Gerizim and began with the blessing: ״ברוך האיש אשר לא יעשה פסל ומסכה״ — these and those answer Amen; they turned their faces toward Mount Ebal and began with the curse: ״ארור האיש אשר יעשה״ and so on — these and those answer Amen, until they finish the blessings and the curses; and afterward they brought the stones and built the altar and plastered it with plaster, and wrote upon them all the words of this Torah in seventy languages, as it says: ״באר היטב״; and they took the stones and came and lodged in their place — up to here in the Mishnah."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:51:37.086384581Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}